The eigenspace is the set of all vectors $v$ such that $Av = 2v$. So, if $v = \begin{bmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{bmatrix}$, then
$$
\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0\\ 4 & 4 & 0\\ 2 & 1 & 2\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}0\\ 2x+2y\\ 2x+y+2z\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}0\\ 0\\ 0\end{bmatrix}
$$
So, the eigenspace is the set of all vectors $v$ such that $2x+2y = 0$ and $2x+y+2z = 0$. This is the same as the set of all vectors $v$ such that $y = -2x$ and $z = -x$. So, the eigenspace is the set of all vectors of the form
$$
\begin{bmatrix}x\\-2x\\-x\end{bmatrix}
=
x
\begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\-1\end{bmatrix}
$$
So, the eigenspace is a one-dimensional subspace of $\mathbb{R}^3$ and the eigenvectors are scalar multiples of $\begin{bmatrix}1\\-2\\-1\end{bmatrix}$.

